---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CTABanner from '../components/sections/CTABanner.astro';

const title = 'How We Work | Certification Consulting Process | ABS Certifications';
const description =
  'How ABS gets organisations certification-ready — from scoping and gap analysis through implementation, training and support during the certification audit. Fixed-price quotes within 24 hours.';
const canonical = new URL('/process', Astro.site).href;

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Can you support us remotely?',
    answer:
      'Most of it, yes. Gap analysis, documentation review, training and audit preparation are routinely delivered fully remotely. Some standards — for example ISO 45001, where site safety must be observed — involve an on-site element, and your certification body may require an on-site audit. We confirm the approach for your scope upfront, so there are no surprises.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What documentation do we need to prepare?',
    answer:
      "It depends on the standard, but typically your policies, key procedures, records that show the system operating (for example management review and internal audit results), and evidence relevant to the standard's requirements. We tell you exactly what is in scope at the gap-analysis stage and help you build whatever is missing, so nothing is discovered for the first time during the audit.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens if we have a non-conformity?',
    answer:
      'A non-conformity is not a failure — it is a finding to be addressed. You implement corrective action within an agreed timeframe and your certification body verifies it. Certification proceeds once findings are closed. We help you plan and evidence the corrective action, and our approach is to surface gaps during our own gap analysis so material issues rarely appear for the first time at the audit.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How quickly can we get the certificate after Stage 2?',
    answer:
      'Once any Stage 2 findings are closed, your certification body makes its certification decision and issues the certificate. For most organisations under 200 employees the full path from kick-off to certificate is around 10–14 weeks; larger or multi-site organisations typically take longer. We give you a firm timeline with your initial quote.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.question,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.answer },
  })),
};
const breadcrumbSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: new URL('/', Astro.site).href },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'How We Work', item: canonical },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbSchema)} />
  </Fragment>

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="margin-bottom:24px">
        <a href="/">Home</a> <span>/</span> <strong>How We Work</strong>
      </nav>
      <div class="page-hero-grid">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:24px">How we work</p>
          <h1>How we get you <em>certification-ready</em>.</h1>
          <p>Rigorous, transparent and practical — from the first scoping call to the certification audit and beyond. Here is exactly what to expect at each stage.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="page-hero-img">
          <img src="/images/process.jpg" alt="ABS certification process planning and audit preparation" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="800" height="600">
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- WHO DOES WHAT -->
  <section class="section section-white" style="padding:72px 0;">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <article class="prose">
        <h2>Who does what</h2>
        <p>It is worth being clear about roles from the outset. <strong>ABS is a consulting and advisory firm.</strong> We prepare your organisation for certification and support you through it. The certificate itself is awarded by an <strong>independent certification body</strong> — a separate organisation that audits you and makes the certification decision. That separation is deliberate: the international rules governing certification bodies (ISO/IEC 17021-1) require the assessment to be impartial and independent of the people who helped build the system.</p>
        <p>In practice that means our job is to make sure the audit holds no surprises. For most organisations under 200 employees, the full path from kick-off to certificate takes around 10–14 weeks; larger or multi-site organisations typically take longer. Every engagement begins with a fixed-price scoping call and a proposal within 24 hours, so you know the timeline and cost before you commit.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE FOUR PHASES -->
  <section class="section section-tint" style="padding:72px 0;">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <article class="prose">
        <h2>The four phases</h2>

        <h3>1. Scoping &amp; quote</h3>
        <p>We start with a scoping call to understand your organisation, the standard you are pursuing, the number of sites, and the complexity of your operations. From that, we send a fixed-price proposal within 24 hours with a firm timeline. Nothing else begins until you are comfortable with the scope and cost. <strong>You'll provide:</strong> a basic picture of your business, sites and any existing certifications.</p>

        <h3>2. Gap analysis</h3>
        <p>We assess your current position against the standard, clause by clause, and produce a clear picture of what already works, what needs strengthening, and what is missing entirely. You get a prioritised plan rather than a list of complaints. This stage is deliberately thorough: it is designed to identify significant weaknesses early and reduce avoidable surprises during the formal audit, though it cannot guarantee the certification body will raise no further findings. It can usually be conducted remotely. <strong>You'll provide:</strong> access to existing policies, procedures and the people who run the processes.</p>

        <h3>3. Implementation &amp; training</h3>
        <p>We work alongside your team to close the gaps — building or reshaping the management system, drafting the policies and procedures that are genuinely missing, and training the people who have to operate it day to day. We also help you run internal audits and a management review, so the system has a track record of actually working before anyone assesses it. <strong>You'll provide:</strong> the people who own the processes, and the time for them to embed the changes.</p>

        <h3>4. Certification audit &amp; beyond</h3>
        <p>Your certification body then carries out its assessment, normally in two parts. <strong>Stage 1</strong> reviews whether the management system is documented and ready — checking policies, scope and readiness. <strong>Stage 2</strong> assesses whether it is genuinely implemented and effective, through interviews, records and observation. We prepare your team and your evidence for both and stay available throughout; if findings are raised, we help you plan and evidence the corrective action. Once findings are closed, the certification body makes its decision and issues the certificate. <strong>You'll provide:</strong> records and people who can show the system working day to day.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- MAINTAINING -->
  <section class="section section-white" style="padding:72px 0;">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <article class="prose">
        <h2>Maintaining your certification</h2>
        <p>A certificate is a living thing. For most ISO standards, certification runs on a three-year cycle, with <strong>surveillance audits</strong> — typically annual — carried out by your certification body in between. These are lighter than the original assessment, but they confirm that your system is still operating and improving rather than drifting after the certificate is on the wall.</p>
        <p>At the end of the cycle, a <strong>recertification audit</strong> renews the certificate for the next period. We can stay involved across that cycle — keeping the system current, helping run internal audits, training new staff, and preparing you for each surveillance visit. If your organisation changes materially — new sites, new activities, a wider scope — we help you work out what that means for your certification and when to raise it. The principle throughout is continuity: certification should reflect how your organisation actually runs, today and over time.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="section section-tint" style="padding:72px 0;">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <article class="prose">
        <h2>Common questions about how we work</h2>
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <Fragment>
            <h3>{f.question}</h3>
            <p>{f.answer}</p>
          </Fragment>
        ))}
        <p>Looking for a specific standard? See, for example, <a href="/services/iso-27001">ISO 27001</a> or <a href="/services/iso-9001">ISO 9001</a> — or <a href="/contact">contact us</a> to talk through your scope.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABanner />
</BaseLayout>
